$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>How the figure travelled upward</h2>
<p>The parties married on 3 December 1997 and have two sons. On 10 January 2025, the Family Court fixed interim maintenance at Rs 75,000 per month. Both sides went to the High Court: the husband to reduce the figure, the wife to enhance it. The High Court dismissed the husband's appeal, allowed the wife's, and doubled the award to Rs 1,50,000 per month — relying on material showing the husband's business turnover of about Rs 6.6 crore, his bank accounts and deposits, and a gift deed executed by him.</p>

<p>The husband carried the matter to the Supreme Court through special leave petitions, which were converted into Civil Appeal Nos. 14206-14207 of 2026 before a Bench of Justice Prashant Kumar Mishra and Justice Shree Chandrashekhar.</p>

<h2>The narrow question on appeal</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>The husband's case</h3><p>The enhanced figure of Rs 1,50,000 per month overstated his means and should be revisited by the Supreme Court on the financial record.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>What the Court asked</h3><p>Not whether it would have fixed the same figure, but whether the High Court's assessment — made on the Rajnesh v. Neha affidavits and the business record — was perverse or unreasonable. It was neither.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Examining the written submissions and the affidavits of assets and liabilities filed under <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> (2021) 2 SCC 324, the Court found the High Court's reasoning in enhancing the maintenance "neither perverse nor unreasonable" in light of the husband's business and investments, and dismissed the civil appeals.</p>

<h2>The contempt petitions: arrears on a clock</h2>
<p>The second half of the order matters as much as the first. By orders of 18 December 2025 and 15 January 2026, the Supreme Court had already directed the husband to pay the accumulated arrears within four months. He did not. The wife filed contempt petitions.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1</strong> — Family Court fixes Rs 75,000 per month (January 2025).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2</strong> — High Court enhances to Rs 1,50,000 per month on the wife's appeal.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3</strong> — Supreme Court orders arrears paid in four months; default follows.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 4</strong> — On the contempt petitions, a final three-month deadline from 16 November 2026, with liberty to file fresh contempt on default.</div>
</div>

<p>Rather than punishing immediately, the Court directed the husband to pay the entire arrears, computed at the High Court rate, within three months. If he defaults again, the wife may file fresh contempt petitions. The petitions were disposed of in those terms — a calibrated use of the contempt jurisdiction that keeps the coercive pressure alive without foreclosing compliance.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should note</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>For claimants:</strong> a well-documented financial picture wins interim maintenance cases. The affidavit discipline of Rajnesh v. Neha — turnover figures, bank deposits, conveyancing documents — is what allowed the enhancement to survive two tiers of challenge. And where arrears pile up despite court deadlines, a contempt petition, rather than another round of execution, may be the pressure point that produces payment.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>For paying spouses, the decision is a caution against litigating the quantum while ignoring the obligation. Each unpaid month compounded the problem: by the time the appeals were dismissed, the arrears themselves had become the subject of judicially supervised deadlines. An interim order remains binding while it is challenged, and the Supreme Court's patience with non-payment is visibly finite.</p>
</div>
